WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.448
Two pointers replace nested loops when moving one boundary forces the other boundary in a single direction.

00:00:07.448 --> 00:00:13.156
Because neither pointer ever steps backward, both traverse the array at most once.

00:00:13.156 --> 00:00:16.080
Total time drops from quadratic to linear.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.109
On a sorted array, start pointers at the minimum and maximum elements.

00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:27.364
If their sum is too small, advance the left pointer; if too large, retreat the right pointer.

00:00:27.364 --> 00:00:31.400
Each comparison eliminates an entire row or column of pairs.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:37.724
For subarray constraints, advance the right pointer step by step to include new elements.

00:00:37.724 --> 00:00:44.559
Whenever the current segment violates the condition, advance the left pointer until validity returns.

00:00:44.559 --> 00:00:49.500
Every right endpoint immediately records its furthest valid left partner.

00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:55.037
Two pointers also synchronize two sorted sequences in a single linear sweep.

00:00:55.037 --> 00:01:00.651
Always advance the pointer holding the smaller element to merge or intersect sets.

00:01:00.651 --> 00:01:04.964
Skip equal neighbors on the fly when unique pairs are required.
